750,315,17767,87,61,17,488,379,61,17,59,3593,16,13,1416,17767,87,284,320,17,488,1124,26888,90,18,5410,88,59,701,1221,271,78045,87,61,17,488,379,61,17,284,17826,17,488,1124,26888,90,18,5410,88,90304,17,488,379,61,17,284,320,19,488,220,19,59,26888,90,18,92,488,220,18,83347,61,17,488,379,61,17,284,320,22,488,220,19,59,26888,90,18,5410,88,61,17,7110,2533,17,13,1416,17767,87,284,320,17,481,1124,26888,90,18,5410,88,59,701,1221,1406,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the minimum value of $x^2 + y^2,$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $x^2 + y^2 = 4xy.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) given the constraint \\(x^2 + y^2 = 4xy\\), we start by manipulating the given equation. First, we rewrite the constraint:\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = 4xy.\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4xy + y^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(x\\). To solve for \\(x\\), we treat \\(y\\) as a constant and use the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4y\\), and \\(c = y^2\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{4y \\pm \\sqrt{(4y)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot y^2}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{4y \\pm \\sqrt{16y^2 - 4y^2}}{2} = \\frac{4y \\pm \\sqrt{12y^2}}{2} = \\frac{4y \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}y}{2} = 2y \\pm \\sqrt{3}y.\\]\n\nSo, the solutions for \\(x\\) are:\n\n\\[x = (2 + \\sqrt{3})y \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = (2 - \\sqrt{3})y.\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these values of \\(x\\) into the expression \\(x^2 + y^2\\) to find the corresponding values of \\(x^2 + y^2\\).\n\n1. If \\(x = (2 + \\sqrt{3})y\\), then\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 + \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 + 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\n2. If \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3})y\\), then\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e837686d0e0ab40129fe92d27239320fe774eb1fe3da97d8deb3514632475109"},"seconds":19.605214595794678,"seed":626539149099774419,"stop":"eos","text":"\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 - \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we compare \\((7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\) and \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\). Clearly, \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\) is smaller because \\(7 - 4\\sqrt{3} < 7 + 4\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\). To find the minimum value, we note that \\(y^2 \\geq 0\\) and the minimum occurs when \\(y \\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(x = 0\\) as well, leading to \\(x^2 + y^2 = 0\\) which does not satisfy the original equation unless \\(x = y = 0\\), a trivial solution). The non-trivial minimum value of the expression is when \\(y^2 = 1\\), giving us:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 - \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we compare \\((7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\) and \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\). Clearly, \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\) is smaller because \\(7 - 4\\sqrt{3} < 7 + 4\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2\\). To find the minimum value, we note that \\(y^2 \\geq 0\\) and the minimum occurs when \\(y \\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(x = 0\\) as well, leading to \\(x^2 + y^2 = 0\\) which does not satisfy the original equation unless \\(x = y = 0\\), a trivial solution). The non-trivial minimum value of the expression is when \\(y^2 = 1\\), giving us:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:580"}
